>> And it does so faster than it increases monster difficulty?

That my friend is the question.

>Until you have the Amulet monster difficulty is determined by the
>average of your depth level and XP level.

Correct.

>  Go deep without
>leveling and mon[s]ters get generated that are meaner than you.

That's true. But there's only one good reason not to level up, and that's
protection. Minetown is pretty much guaranteed not to be below level 8
and that's rare. A buff pet can take care of most of the riff raff generated
until you get to Minetown.

>Level up and stay shallow and monsters get generated that are
>easy for you to take out.

But you get a cascading effect due to levelling up, even if you stay still
on the same level.

If your AC is out of whack, the first time you encounter killer bees or
soldier ants, you're going to have a rough time of it, even if you're on
dungeon level 3 or 4.

>The reasons for playing the protection racket and the reasons
>for leveling up don't have much overlap.

True. But they play really well together if you do them serially. THe
PR is a temporary state of game play. You play that semi pacifist role
just long enough to get the huge AC bump. Then attack away.

I've found that it's really hard work not levelling up. By the time you've
taken out a shrieker, a floating eye, and a couple of gnome zombies, you're
already a 4th level character. You can get from level 1 to level 6 is a couple
of hundred turns.

But the protection AC will assist you in each and every melee encounter you
have in the game. All the way to ascension if you can refrain from murdering
anyone. It's a huge boost.

Also buffing up the pet helps well into midgame.

My first character I ascended was a wizard. I didn't learn how to play a PR
early game until after I had done that. I know now that if I had known how
to place the PR game, that 1st ascension would have come a lot sooner.

>  Pick one of the two strategies, stick with it and be happy.

Even better blend the two strategies and be much much better.

PR builds characters that can better handle the long haul.

